Sval: web pages scroll with thin scrollbars by default

Sval's own scrollers draw a thin scrollbar on a clear track. The UA sheet
gives every page the same default, below any author style, so a page that
styles its scrollbars keeps its own.

diff --git a/layout/style/res/ua.css b/layout/style/res/ua.css
index 037c5e20b4..78edc3d69e 100644
--- a/layout/style/res/ua.css
+++ b/layout/style/res/ua.css
@@ -9,6 +9,29 @@
             them
 */
 
+/* Sval: every scroller is thin, with a grey thumb on a clear track. The thumb
+ * is neutral because the root's colour does not say how dark the page behind
+ * it is. Being in the UA sheet, both lose to any author declaration.
+ * `scrollbar-color` is inherited, so the root carries it, and
+ * `scrollbar-width` is not, so every element does. The rule for colour sits
+ * outside forced colours, where UA declarations still apply
+ * (`ignored_when_colors_disabled` drops only author ones). Windows 11's thin
+ * overlay strip draws its thumb as a line until hovered
+ * (`ScrollbarDrawingWin11`), which a translucent grey leaves faint. */
+*|* {
+  scrollbar-width: thin;
+}
+
+@media not (forced-colors) {
+  :root {
+    scrollbar-color: rgb(128 128 128 / 0.6) transparent;
+
+    @media (-moz-platform: windows) and (-moz-overlay-scrollbars) {
+      scrollbar-color: rgb(128 128 128) transparent;
+    }
+  }
+}
+
